WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.850
One, two.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.880
I often get asked the question
"Are HBCUs still relevant?"

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.240
and I think that is a ridiculous question,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.803
that's an asinine question.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:23.610
The question is, how
could you take so little

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:24.960
and do so much.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.210
How could you take so little

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.630
and produce some of the greatest graduates

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.233
our country has ever known.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.520
We were not allowed to attend
the majority institutions.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.250
We had to come to our own institutions,

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.884
the historical black
colleges and universities,

00:00:41.884 --> 00:00:44.465
but to be successful as a
university requires funding

00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:46.753
and this was one thing withheld from TSU.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.760
We cannot talk about fairness and equality

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.902
if you do not provide the
equal amount of funding

00:00:55.902 --> 00:00:58.570
that you're required by federal law,

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:03.487
it speaks louder than any words
about equality and justice.

00:01:06.297 --> 00:01:07.130
This has been happening for decades

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.340
and decades and decades,

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.603
it is an example of institutional racism.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.370
The states were supposed to
give these schools money,

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.803
and quite frankly, they didn't.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.060
How many more students could

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.890
these institutions graduate

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.120
if they had received their proper funding.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.040
How many more research opportunities

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.750
would have been made at Tennessee state?

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.860
How many more lives
would have been changed

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.292
if Tennessee state did not
have this huge and huge amount

00:01:35.292 --> 00:01:37.373
owed them by the state of Tennessee?

00:01:39.217 --> 00:01:42.260
We don't want to continue to promise

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.787
to do what was in law

00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:47.660
and continue to just say no.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.333
That was done too many
times to Tennessee state.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.890
My name is Janet Lorin
and I write about finances

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.784
of higher ed here at Bloomberg.

00:02:04.784 --> 00:02:09.780
I decided to look into about
20 public schools in the south

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.370
when I heard about how
they have not been funded

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.063
for decades and decades from their states.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.030
An important job at Bloomberg
is following the money

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:21.470
and it was clear in this case

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:23.320
there wasn't as much money to follow.

00:02:24.396 --> 00:02:27.000
HBCUs use are very important historically,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.560
they launched the careers of luminaries

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.550
such as Martin Luther king, Jr.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.920
Thurgood Marshall, Toni Morrison,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.240
Vice President Kamala
Harris and Oprah Winfrey.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.103
Cause I didn't go to Stanford.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.770
I went to Tennessee State University.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:45.490
We have a lot of first
generation college students.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.210
They come to Tennessee State University,

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.182
go to other HBCUs because
they want to break

00:02:50.182 --> 00:02:52.450
their cycle of poverty.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:55.420
Education is the first step
to breaking that cycle,

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.710
to live in a new economic base.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.283
That's what we're seeking
and that's why HBCUs

00:03:00.283 --> 00:03:01.977
have become so important.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.820
During the civil war in 1862,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.030
the United States under the leadership

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.760
of President Abraham Lincoln created

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.970
the land grant system of universities.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.560
And that essentially created

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.070
a type of university that was public.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.750
Previously people in the United
States only went to college

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.320
if they were extremely rich,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.660
think of Harvard, Yale and Princeton

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.853
or they were going to become a minister.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.800
The government decided to help
create public universities

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.750
where students could learn about

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.103
agriculture, military, farming.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.308
After the civil war, during reconstruction

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:48.620
the states in the south
did not want to educate

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.960
former slaves and black students.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.210
So the United States government came up

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.750
with a second moral act in 1890

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.690
and they said if there is
going to be discrimination

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.190
in admissions, that there should be

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.550
a second land grant university.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.050
So this gave students the opportunity

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.090
to be educated at another place

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.550
if the University of Georgia
or the University of Florida,

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.000
or the University of Tennessee didn't want

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.793
to educate black students.

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:19.590
There's the federal
funds that had to be met

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.090
by the state, then there's a
state funding that's provided
to land grant institutions.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.120
And in both of those areas
we have fallen short.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.512
TSU got the short end of the stick.

00:04:30.512 --> 00:04:34.804
I get calls regularly from
other HBCUs that are land grant

00:04:34.804 --> 00:04:37.037
and the same matching
funds are owed to them.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:44.830
There are new developments

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.520
in the death of George Floyd,

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.580
the unarmed black man in Minneapolis

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.370
who died in police custody.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.350
Post the death of George Floyd

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.930
these issues are being discussed

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.000
in the Tennessee state legislature.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.690
Several legislative committee were held

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.850
over several months to discuss

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.930
what Tennessee State
University didn't get.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:09.340
And I think that is a turning
point and that hasn't happened

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.520
in other southern states before.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.870
Some legislators say they
didn't know about it.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.265
Good afternoon. I would like to call the

00:05:19.265 --> 00:05:22.590
Joint Land-Grant Institution
Funding Study Committee

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.423
to order.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:29.360
Harold Love is a state
legislator in Tennessee.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.930
He went to Tennessee state,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.540
his parents met at Tennessee state,

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.400
his grandmother went to Tennessee state.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.663
She became a teacher, his
wife went to Tennessee state.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.030
Last year we were, the legislature,

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:47.210
charged with discovering if
there were funding inequities

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.600
for the two land grants
we have here in Tennessee

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.870
and I along with Senator Richard Briggs

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.560
were charged as the
chairs of this committee.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.210
And so our task is to investigate to see

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.280
if Tennessee State University
may have been underfunded

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:01.700
by the state of Tennessee.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.570
This is going to be a deep dive

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:07.370
into land grants and the
benefits, how we'll fund it.

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:08.600
And I really hope that

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.320
we come up with some
idea of going forward,

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.320
how we can be a model for other states.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.370
We looked into the situation
and discovered that indeed

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.340
Tennessee state was being
underfunded at that moment

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.750
by at least $1 million per year.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.800
And that's a state requirement
that is supposed to match

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:32.090
federal funds for the land grant funding.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:35.070
What we found was the
primarily white institutions

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:36.797
always received the funding
and the predominantly

00:06:36.797 --> 00:06:39.318
black HBCU institutions

00:06:39.318 --> 00:06:42.903
did not receive their funding.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.100
We have funding mechanisms in place

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:49.320
that creates a difference in between them,

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:50.473
that is discrimination.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:58.234
We began with a study of about
2000 to 2016, those years,

00:06:58.234 --> 00:07:00.982
discovered that there was $37 million

00:07:00.982 --> 00:07:04.360
that was not funded to TSU that time.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.380
But this still was not the major piece.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.650
I knew that there was more there
than just this $37 million,

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.423
but we did not have the data.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.900
I needed to first go and
look in the budget books

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.990
to find out if the state

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.940
had put anything in the budget books

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:23.940
for Tennessee state.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.760
So I go up to the state
archives library that we have

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.263
in the building and I start with 1957,

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:37.240
open the book to see where
Tennessee state's deficiency is,

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:40.421
I want to see how much they
have not funded Tennessee state.

00:07:40.421 --> 00:07:44.770
So I noticed that there's
no entry, like literally

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.993
Tennessee state is not listed
for their land grant funding.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:49.703
So I said, okay,

00:07:49.703 --> 00:07:52.113
this is one year when there
was nothing in the budget.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.560
So I said, well let me
look in the next year

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.590
and see if there's, maybe
this was just one year

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:58.900
when it wasn't there.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.660
I looked in the next year,
there's no money there.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.653
I skipped ahead and said, well,

00:08:03.653 --> 00:08:04.486
maybe that was just that decade,

00:08:04.486 --> 00:08:08.010
but come back to 1950s,
but it goes to 1960s.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.113
There's no money there.

00:08:09.113 --> 00:08:11.050
So I decided to spend the whole day there

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:13.470
looking through all the budget books

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:17.420
from the 1957 period to 2007.

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:19.831
And in each instance what I discovered was

00:08:19.831 --> 00:08:23.690
there was not an entry in the budget books

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:24.690
for Tennessee state.

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:29.040
And unfortunately my
worst fears were confirmed

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.270
by the house budget office.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:34.160
They too did their research and discovered

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.450
that Tennessee state was not allocated

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:38.173
their funding in those decades.

00:08:39.013 --> 00:08:42.003
That's not even the word land
grant in the budget book.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:50.500
My father served in
legislature from 1968 to 1994.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.380
And upon winning my election,

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.010
I went to my parents' house

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.810
and was going through my father's office,

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:58.300
just trying to find some things

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.840
that I may be able to take to my office.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.180
I discovered this book called

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:03.890
the Special Financial Needs
of Negro Universities.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:06.140
At that time didn't look into and read

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.700
because I'm gathering documents.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.790
I'm trying to figure out
how I can make the point

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.500
a bit more salient for
my colleagues downtown.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.510
It's not getting much traction.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:21.360
And then I get this sense
of this voice saying to me,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.003
"Read the report."

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:25.630
So I read the report.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:28.050
In this report it details
how Tennessee state

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:30.500
had been underfunded and how they had

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.830
appointed a legislative committee in 1970

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:35.340
to investigate this.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.060
But nothing was done.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:40.860
Not one recommendation was
followed that the committee made.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.280
But something's wrong if Harold Love, Sr

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.600
was making his recommendations,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.440
in 2021 Harold Love, Jr is having to make

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:49.970
those same ones, something's wrong.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:54.873
It disturbs me that 50 years later,

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.080
I'm making the same argument.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.790
That document became I think
the catalyst for getting

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.800
Tennessee state their state
match funding that year,

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.470
but also became the catalyst
for us having documentation

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:11.220
for this new committee
that we were starting.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:16.220
The unfortunate reality
is the state did not value

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.930
Tennessee state as much as the

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.700
University of Tennessee at Knoxville.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.330
And some may view that as a
harsh statement that I'm making,

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:24.730
but I'm looking at the data.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:28.090
Thank you for the opportunity
to present here today.

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:29.700
So during the hearings,

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:32.770
some budget analyst
looked through the books

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.450
and came up with a couple of calculations

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:38.860
from $150 million to $544 million

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:42.070
that could be owed to Tennessee state.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:44.203
So the question is what happens next?

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:48.137
North Nashville, as we mentioned,

00:10:48.137 --> 00:10:50.200
a hard hit area by
Monday night's tornadoes.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.840
You walk around the
campus of Tennessee state

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:56.620
and you see the facilities
some were destroyed

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:59.690
by a tornado in March of 2020

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.690
and the agriculture dean tells me

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:04.160
they likely will not be rebuilt

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.860
for at least two years after that.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:10.070
So you get the sense of what potentially

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:12.430
could have been if the
school had more money.

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:14.840
And as we pan across here to
the more agriculture center,

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:17.477
these are the greenhouses here at TSU,

00:11:17.477 --> 00:11:18.310
you can see all the panels

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:20.875
on top that would cover
those, just flew off.

00:11:20.875 --> 00:11:23.020
The greenhouses and a
bunch of other facilities

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:25.430
they were completely now bulldozed down.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.410
These were beautiful greenhouses,

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:32.010
where we were doing a lot
of outstanding research work

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.730
and now you got a bunch of rubble here.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:39.637
We lost several facilities
in our research station,

00:11:39.637 --> 00:11:42.220
the Nashville Research Station.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.500
It's a big disappointment.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.860
You can see if you talk
to any of our faculty,

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:51.260
you'll get more frustration
than I can express really

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.290
and then if the university
is taking two years

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:55.620
to build something that
we lost in a tornado

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.880
that doesn't generate
much confidence either.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:02.270
When you look at the poultry
house on TSU's campus

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:05.010
you see a building that was
destroyed by the tornado,

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:06.780
but one that is still left,

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:08.560
but it is in bad condition because

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.737
that building was built in 1970.

00:12:10.737 --> 00:12:13.780
If you bring a Falcon member here and say,

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.703
we want you to do research
here, they see this equipment

00:12:16.703 --> 00:12:19.290
there's no way they're
going to agree to come here

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:21.720
and be a Falcon member to do research

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.100
with equipment this old.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.914
No, you're driving them away.

00:12:24.914 --> 00:12:26.646
This is just an example, though.

00:12:26.646 --> 00:12:31.030
We got all of our buildings are so dated.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:32.100
We can't attract them.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.450
The high schools have better
facilities these days.

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:36.283
Wow.
Yeah.

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:42.210
So you create this environment where

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:44.200
you have deferred
maintenance on buildings,

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:46.520
you have lowest scholarship
offerings for students,

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:48.640
you have lower salaries for teachers

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:50.280
and you most certainly have lower

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:53.180
endowment levels for your university.

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:57.270
It is now our responsibility as stewards

00:12:57.270 --> 00:13:00.600
of state government to ensure that

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:03.413
this state institution is properly funded.

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:20.140
TSU has gotten more money from
the state in recent years,

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:22.810
they've been able to get better resources

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:25.430
and they've been able to
get more competitive grants.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:27.930
But the question is
what could these schools

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:29.920
have looked like if historically

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.693
they had gotten a lot more money.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:37.400
The arrearage amount that
the state of Tennessee

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:41.850
owes Tennessee state
represents generations

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:44.612
of African-Americans that
could have been educated.

00:13:44.612 --> 00:13:48.100
It represents countless
opportunities for the community

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:52.180
to be enhanced because of
TSU's presence in Nashville.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:54.312
When he mentions the word arrearage,

00:13:54.312 --> 00:13:57.680
that's looking back, we've
already been over that.

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:00.638
We have to look at
where we're going to be,

00:14:00.638 --> 00:14:01.850
what we're going to do going forward,

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:05.490
not trying to remedy completely
something in the past.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:08.680
We can't look backwards and go forward.

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:11.880
We don't need to be trying to operate

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:13.600
looking in the rear view mirror.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.890
I hear some of our members
saying that we can't look back.

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:20.035
I do think that we have
to take in consideration

00:14:20.035 --> 00:14:23.660
some of the things that
have happened in the past

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.930
in order for us to build on the future.

00:14:26.930 --> 00:14:31.610
To this year's HBCU graduates
and to all your loved ones,

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:35.300
who've helped you reach
this day, congratulations.

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:38.020
The Biden administration has earmarked

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:40.950
and given money to HBCUs in a way

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:42.930
that we really haven't ever seen before.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:46.257
The administration has
made HBCUs a priority.

00:14:46.257 --> 00:14:49.454
And while in some cases
that doesn't make up for

00:14:49.454 --> 00:14:51.738
decades and decades in the past,

00:14:51.738 --> 00:14:54.315
it is a new page going forward.

00:14:54.315 --> 00:14:58.470
It's not just a dollar figure
because it goes beyond that.

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:01.450
It affects the lives of the community.

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:03.430
A grave injustice took place.

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:06.480
But right now we want them
to make the correction.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:09.643
Causeit's never too
late to do what's right.

